Hyuuga-no-Kami Ibun

Episode 47: The Fall of Shigisan Castle

&#x27;—At last the Oda host breaks the main gate. Men and horses come flooding into the castle, a whole angry sea with hooves. Uemon-no-suke stands before them without flinching. Courage is present in full quantity. Men on his side are not. Here the shortage becomes final.&#x27;

&#x27;Three men he strikes down, four. Still he turns his head, looking for the next. From the Oda ranks shrinking before him, one Sasaki comes running—a retainer of Koretou Hyuuga—and breaks his spear.&#x27;

&#x27;Draw his sword? Where is the time? The common soldiers see their chance. Now, now! Spear after spear, belly and shoulder, and the fortune of Matsunaga&#x27;s heir is finished. Sasaki takes his head. Thus Uemon-no-suke Hisamichi meets his end, aged thirty-five. No death verse, it is said.&#x27;

&#x27;When word comes that Uemon-no-suke has fallen, the defenders&#x27; unity also falls. Some change banners and turn their spears. Others throw away armor and helmet and run. One after another, the commitment is leaving. The besiegers set fire to the second and third wards; even the determined men abandon hope and take their own lives, keeping at least their bodies from the enemy.&#x27;

&#x27;Alas, Matsunaga Danjou-no-jou! From the top of the inner ward he watches this business and knows his final hour has arrived. Below, armies mass like clouds. Their commander is Akita-jou-no-suke, eldest son of that hated Nobunaga. Danjou sees the man&#x27;s standard, and his teeth grind.&#x27;

&#x27;Defeat, fine. Death, also fine. But going away without striking back even once? Absolutely not. Danjou lifts his tea kettle and displays it to the Oda men. Akita-jou-no-suke jumps up before thinking. Beside him, Hashiba Chikuzen is also in full alarm.&#x27;

&#x27;That strange kettle, looking like a spider crouching on the earth, is the famous treasure Oda Naifu has long desired. For this kettle, Naifu even offered to pardon Matsunaga&#x27;s two rebellions. Two rebellions on one kettle: such was its value.&#x27;

&#x27;Akita-jou-no-suke shouts desperately for him to stop. Danjou has no interest in stopping. He laughs his contempt at full volume and recites his final verse.&#x27;

&#x27;—Darubushi, Adura, Uru, Baakuru—&#x27;

&#x27;Was that verse itself the spark? An enormous explosion shakes the castle. Smoke buries everything around.&#x27;

&#x27;The Oda men watch the whole business without a word.&#x27;
